<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif;">
    <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
        <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
    </div>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Colors of Things that Go</td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Red Bike.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b><br>Mediation: Text: Draw a single picture that represents a simple story or event.<br>Communication: Lead a simple classroom activity like a lineup or cleanup using gestures and basic words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b><br>Mediation: Can use gestures or drawings to describe transportation vehicles in the classroom (e.g., drawing a picture of a car or miming the act of driving a car to describe the word "car").<br>Can clarify responses by pointing to pictures of transportation vehicles when needed.<br>Can use basic words and body language to explain how transportation vehicles are used during classroom activities (e.g., pretending to drive by moving hands like holding a steering wheel).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
                <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows large flashcards of "car," "bus," and "bike" in different colors (e.g., "red bike," "blue bus," "green car"). The teacher says the word and children repeat chorally, using TPR (e.g., miming driving a car, riding a bike). Teacher asks, "What color is this bike?" while pointing to a red bike. Students respond with a single word or a gesture. (Grammar: Color + noun, WH-questions; Vocabulary: car, bus, bike, green, blue, red; Pragmatic: Answering questions with one-word/gestures).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a yellow car. "Look! This is a yellow car." The teacher then quickly draws a simple yellow car on the board, saying, "I draw a yellow car. This is a yellow car." The teacher repeats for a blue bus, drawing it while saying "This is a blue bus." The teacher emphasizes the initial sounds /k/ in 'car' and /b/ in 'bus' while modeling. The teacher also shows how to clarify by pointing directly to the drawing or flashcard. (Grammar: Color + noun, Present simple; Vocabulary: car, bus, yellow, blue; Pronunciation: /k/, /b/; Pragmatic: Describing objects, Clarifying responses).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b><br><li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a red bike flashcard and a drawing of a red bike. "Is this a green bike?" (Expected answer: "No!" or shaking head).</li><li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a drawing of a bus. "What is this?" (Expected answer: "Bus!" or pointing to the drawing).</li></p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Colors of Things that Go".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, visually-rich story presented as a series of large, simple pictures on the board or projector, showing different colored vehicles moving (e.g., a "red car goes zoom," a "blue bus stops"). Each picture depicts a simple event that can be represented by a drawing. (Genre: Picture Story; Register: Simple narrative; Format: Visual sequence).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students understand simple descriptions of vehicles and their colors, identifying them by pointing and basic words. They begin to grasp how a drawing can represent a simple action or object.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b><br><li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> After showing a picture of "A green truck goes," the teacher asks, "What color is the truck?" (Students say "Green!" or point to green). "Does the truck go or stop?" (Students mime "go" or "stop").</li><li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Gestures):</b> Teacher says, "The yellow taxi is big." (Students give a thumbs up for true or thumbs down for false, referring to the picture). Teacher shows a picture of "A purple bike." Teacher says, "It is a blue bike." (Students show thumbs down).</li><li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Teacher shows two pictures: one with a "red car" and another with a "blue bus." Teacher says, "Point to the red car, please." (Sociolinguistic: Norms of politeness; Pragmatic: Answering questions about objects, Pointing; Vocabulary: car, bus, red, blue, big, go, stop).</li></p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice describing vehicles using "Color + noun" and "It is a..." or "This is a..." through drawing, coloring, and simple gesturing. They learn to mediate information by drawing to represent concepts and using gestures to explain actions like "drive" or "ride." (Grammar: Color + noun, Present simple, Demonstratives; Vocabulary: car, bus, bike, green, blue, red, yellow, drive, ride; Pronunciation: /k/, /b/, /y/; Pragmatic: Describing objects).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b><br><li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Guided Drawing & Coloring:</b> The teacher provides worksheets with outlines of a car, a bus, and a bike. The teacher calls out a color and a vehicle, e.g., "Color the car red!" Students color the car red. Then, the teacher models writing "This is a red car." below the drawing. Students trace or attempt to copy the sentence. This activity helps mediate the connection between the spoken word, the color, and the visual representation.</li><li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Vehicle Action Charades (Mediation via Gestures):</b> The teacher says a vehicle and an action, e.g., "drive a bus," "ride a bike," "go in a car." Students perform the corresponding mime. The teacher then asks, "How do you 'drive'?" and mimes driving. Students imitate. This mediates the meaning of verbs through physical action. The teacher can say, "Please, show me 'ride a bike'." (Sociolinguistic: Politeness; Vocabulary: drive, ride, go, stop, car, bus, bike).</li><li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Draw and Guess (Partner Mediation):</b> In pairs, one student draws a simple vehicle of a specific color (e.g., a green truck) without showing their partner. The partner guesses by saying, "Is it a red car?" or "What color is it?" The drawing student can nod/shake head, or point to their drawing if the partner guesses the color/vehicle correctly. The goal is for the drawing to mediate the information to the partner. (Vocabulary: all vehicle and color words; Grammar: WH-questions, Present simple).</li></p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Colors of Things that Go".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative "Vehicle Drawing Game" presentation. Students will work individually to complete their project drawing, and then present it in small groups or to the class, using their drawings as a mediation tool.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure (21st Century Skills Project):</b><br><li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Project Creation:</b> Each student receives a blank sheet of paper and drawing/coloring supplies. The teacher assigns or allows students to choose one vehicle (car, bus, or bike) and one color. For instance, "Draw a blue bus!" or "Draw a red bike." Students draw and color their chosen vehicle. (Specific Objective: Draw a single picture).</li><li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Labeling (Mediation: Text):</b> The teacher helps students write a simple label sentence for their drawing, such as "This is a blue bus." or "It is a red bike." The teacher circulates, providing individual assistance with spelling and sentence structure as needed. This directly addresses the mediation objective of drawing representing text. (Grammar: Present simple, Demonstratives; Vocabulary: all target vehicles and colors).</li><li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Presentation (Mediation: Communication):</b> Students sit in small groups (3-4). Each student takes turns holding up their drawing and saying, "This is a red bike!" or "It's a green car!" They are encouraged to use gestures to show how the vehicle works (e.g., miming 'drive' for a car, 'ride' for a bike). If a group member doesn't understand, the presenter clarifies by pointing to the drawing or making the gesture more pronounced. The teacher prompts with "What color is it?" or "Show me 'drive'!" (Learning Outcome: Use gestures/drawings to describe, clarify responses by pointing, use body language to explain usage; Pragmatic: Describing objects, Answering questions).</li></p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist (focusing on mediation skills) and product assessment (the labeled drawing).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student presents their "Vehicle Drawing Game" artwork to the teacher or a peer. They must clearly state the vehicle and its color, using the target sentence structure (e.g., "This is a red bike."). The student must also use a gesture or mime to demonstrate how the vehicle moves or is used (e.g., mime driving for a car). The teacher (or peer) asks a clarifying question, such as "Is it a blue car?" and the student must point to their drawing or shake their head appropriately. (Learning Outcome: Use gestures/drawings to describe, clarify responses by pointing, use basic words and body language to explain). (Specific Objective: Draw a picture, lead a simple classroom activity like cleanup using gestures and basic words if applicable during transitions).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b><br><li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Drawing:</b> Is the vehicle identifiable?</li><li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Does the student use "color + noun" (e.g., "red bike") and verbs like "drive" or "ride" through gestures?</li><li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Sentence:</b> Is the accompanying label or verbal description understandable (e.g., "This is a red bike.")?</li><li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Effective Gestures/Mime:</b> Does the student use appropriate body language to explain the vehicle's use?</li><li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarification Ability:</b> Can the student clarify by pointing to their drawing when prompted?</li></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b><br><li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I draw a red car?" (Students circle Yes/No on a small paper or give thumbs up/down).</li><li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I show my friend how a bus goes?" (Students mime "go" or show a gesture).</li></p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b><br><li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is your favorite vehicle to draw and color?" (Students can point to their drawing or a flashcard).</li><li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you show me how a train goes?" (Students use gestures/sound).</li></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b><br><li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students mediate information through drawing and gestures? (e.g., clarity of instructions, engagement with the drawing activity, peer interaction).</li><li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for future lessons to further develop their mediation skills, especially for clarifying and representing information non-verbally? (e.g., more practice with mime, structured peer feedback, simpler sentence frames for labeling).</li></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the theme "That's a Red Bike." The next theme will build upon students' ability to describe objects and use simple sentences, possibly introducing more complex descriptions or actions within a new scenario, maintaining the use of drawings and gestures as a crucial comprehension and production support for early learners.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>